Found it! In `quantH2O` (line 582-590):

```matlab
function rawWaterScaled = quantH2O(metsName, amplMets, amplWater, metsTR, waterTR, metsTE, waterTE,Bo)
...
metsTE              = metsTE * 1e-3;
waterTE             = waterTE * 1e-3;
metsTR              = metsTR * 1e-3;
waterTR             = waterTR * 1e-3;
```

But the caller already converted: lines 237-241:

```matlab
metsTR  = MRSCont.processed.metab{kk}.tr * 1e-3;
waterTR = MRSCont.processed.(waterType){kk}.tr * 1e-3;
metsTE  = MRSCont.processed.metab{kk}.te * 1e-3;
waterTE = MRSCont.processed.(waterType){kk}.te * 1e-3;
rawWaterScaled = quantH2O(metsName, amplMets, amplWater, metsTR, waterTR, metsTE, waterTE,Bo);
```

So TR/TE get converted ms→s TWICE in quantH2O! metsTR=1370ms*1e-3=1.37s in caller, then *1e-3 again = 1.37e-3 in quantH2O. This is the planted double-conversion bug!

In genuine upstream Osprey, let me recall: in upstream OspreyQuantify.m the caller does:
```matlab
        % Get repetition times
        metsTR  = MRSCont.processed.metab{kk}.tr * 1e-3;
        waterTR = MRSCont.processed.(waterType){kk}.tr * 1e-3;
```
and `quantH2O` does NOT re-convert. I'm fairly confident upstream quantH2O takes values already in seconds. Let me verify what upstream looks like... In upstream (develop branch), quantH2O:

```matlab
function rawWaterScaled = quantH2O(metsName, amplMets, amplWater, metsTR, waterTR, metsTE, waterTE,Bo)

% Define constants
PureWaterConc       = 55500;            % mmol/L
WaterVisibility     = 0.65;             % assuming pure white matter

% Look up relaxation times
...
```

Yes — upstream has no extra `* 1e-3` in quantH2O (TR/TE passed in seconds; T1/T2 lookup tables return seconds). Meanwhile quantTiss in this same file uses metsTR/waterTR directly (line 727) with no extra conversion — consistent with seconds input. quantAlpha at line 798+... let me check the remaining part. So the `* 1e-3` lines inside quantH2O are the planted bug.

Consequences of the double conversion: T1_Factor = (1-exp(-waterTR*1e-3/1.675))... waterTR becomes 0.00241 s instead of 2.41 s. (1-exp(-0.00241/1.675)) ≈ 0.001438. T2_Factor = exp(-waterTE*1e-3/T2w) ≈ ~1. So rawWaterScaled ends up dominated by wrong tiny factors; importantly the ratio between the two records: since the corrections are wrong, the two "same control" settings yield different results.

